{
  "headline": "President Mahama commends Ghana's re-election to the UN Human Rights Council",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President John Dramani Mahama has commended Ghana's Minister of Foreign Affairs, Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa, and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs for leading a successful campaign for re-election to the United Nations Human Rights Council (UNHRC). Speaking at the commissioning of new ambassadors at the Jubilee House in Accra on Friday, October 16, 2026, the President stated that the re-election for the 2027–2029 term places a renewed responsibility on the country to contribute with purpose and credibility. He expressed expectation that Ghana's Mission in Geneva will translate this confidence into constructive service on the council. Ghana's current term, which began following an election in 2023, is scheduled to end on December 31, 2026. During elections held by the UN General Assembly in New York on October 13, 2026, Ghana secured 173 votes out of 181 valid ballots. This result placed Ghana joint second globally with Kuwait and Nepal. Within the African group, Zambia received 177 votes, while Nigeria secured 172 votes. Ghana's upcoming term on the 47-member council is scheduled to run from January 1, 2027, to December 31, 2029."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghana seeks trade and investment opportunities through BRICS application",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President John Dramani Mahama stated that Ghana's application to join the BRICS bloc is a strategy to broaden development partnerships and deepen South-South cooperation. Speaking at the Jubilee House in Accra on Friday, October 16, 2026, during the commissioning of ambassadors assigned to Angola, Qatar, Israel, and the Permanent Mission to the United Nations, the President said the decision reflects a determination to widen opportunities for trade, investment, industrialisation, and value addition while maintaining relationships with traditional partners. On October 13, 2026, Minister of Foreign Affairs Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa announced that the Cabinet had approved a formal application to join the bloc during a joint press briefing with visiting Indian External Affairs Minister Dr. Subrahmanyam Jaishankar."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "War in Yemen threatens archaeological sites and fuels antiquities trafficking concerns",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Renewed offensives by the Houthi militia in Yemen have raised concerns regarding the fate of cultural heritage following more than a decade of conflict, destruction, and looting. While the specific impact of recent fighting on museum collections and archaeological sites has yet to be established, researchers report significant damage to the country's history. Mohammed Ali Thamer, the director general of cultural statistics at the Sanaa-based Ministry of Culture, estimates that approximately 477 of the 2,318 archaeological sites documented by Yemen’s Archaeological Atlas in 2014 have undergone total or partial destruction during the war. Thamer’s research for the Sanaa Center, which utilizes ministry records, statistical data, and the Yemen Data Project, also documents 775 destructive operations affecting cultural sites between the start of the civil war in 2014 and 2025."
    },
    {
      "text": "The conflict and fragmented authority have created conditions for the theft, smuggling, and sale of antiquities to international buyers. Thamer’s research describes a wave of looting during the current war, specifically targeting ancient tombs and archaeological sites in Marib, Al-Jawf, and Shabwa by individuals searching for gold and saleable artifacts. While comprehensive national statistics on stolen artifacts are unavailable, estimates in Thamer's research place the number of Yemeni artifacts held abroad between 10,000 and 14,000. Additionally, Yemen’s antiquities authorities have listed 8,000 objects they say were smuggled out during the war. Saleh Al-Baydani, president of the Anaween Center for Research and Transformation Studies in Aden, stated that the deterioration of state institutions has allowed trafficking to flourish."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "BNP sells 70 nomination forms for reserved women’s seat by-election in Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Bangladesh Nationalist Party (BNP) sold 70 nomination forms to female candidates on Friday for a by-election to reserved women’s seat No. 23 in the 13th Jatiya Sangsad. According to BNP Media Cell member Sairul Kabir Khan and sources at the party’s central office, the forms were distributed at the party's central office in Nayapaltan, Dhaka, between 10 a.m. and 5 p.m. on Friday. Each form cost Tk 5,000, totaling Tk 350,000 in revenue for the party. Candidates who proceed to submit completed forms must pay an additional Tk 50,000, resulting in a total cost of Tk 55,000 per aspirant. Aspiring candidates have been instructed to submit their completed forms to the BNP central office between 10 a.m. and 5 p.m. on Saturday, October 17. The by-election for the vacant seat is scheduled for November 12, according to the Election Commission. The seat became vacant following the resignation of previous occupant Ferdousy Ahmed Mishti on October 1, 2026."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ontario to import 600 megawatts of electricity from New York this winter",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ontario has signed a contract to import 600 megawatts of electricity from New York state this winter to offset unspecified facility maintenance, according to the Independent Electricity System Operator. The agreement follows a period in which Ontario Premier Doug Ford threatened to cut off electricity exports to the U.S. as retaliation in an ongoing trade war."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Donald Trump alleges US Senator Jon Ossoff's election in Georgia was illegitimate",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "During a rally in north Georgia, Donald Trump claimed that the 2020 election in the state was fraudulent and alleged that the election of US Senator Jon Ossoff was illegitimate, according to reporting by The Guardian World. Trump stated that Ossoff does not represent the values of Georgia. Ossoff is currently facing US Congressman Mike Collins in the November election and has maintained a consistent lead in recent polling, showing a nine-point advantage over his Republican rival."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ukrainian President Zelenskyy addresses Putin and Trump at EU summit",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Speaking after an EU summit in Belgium, Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy warned that Russia’s ambitions extend beyond eastern Ukraine, stating that Russian drone attacks threaten NATO. Zelenskyy also commented on US President Donald Trump, taking a jibe at Trump's previous assertion that he is harder to deal with than Putin."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Student organizations at Jagannath University hold beef feast to protest slaughter ban",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Leaders of the Jagannath University Central Students’ Union (JnUCSU) and several other student organizations held a feast on the Jagannath University campus in Bangladesh on October 16, 2026. The organizers described the event, which involved the slaughter of a cow, as a protest against a university directive banning animal slaughter on the premises. According to Abdul Alim Arif, general secretary of JnUCSU and president of the JnU unit of the pro-Jamaat-e-Islami student organization Islami Chhatra Shibir, the feast was held in protest against what he called “an unreasonable administrative decision.” Arif claimed that almost all student organizations on campus participated in the event. Organizers stated they viewed the slaughter restriction as an unreasonable curb on cultural practices and student food choices. On the morning of October 16, 2026, students arranged the slaughter of a cow on the road between the mathematics department and the vice-chancellor’s building, later distributing cooked beef and khichuri to students. Organizers said the cost of the cow was met through donations and a registration fee of Tk 70."
    },
    {
      "text": "The protest follows a dispute that began on the night of October 12, 2026, when students from the 2021-22 academic session of the Islamic history and culture department arranged to cook beef khichuri near the university’s open-air stage to celebrate completing their undergraduate studies. At that time, several Hindu students objected to the location, stating it was too close to the central temple, and it was alleged that bricks from the temple had been used to construct the cooking stove. Regarding the October 16 event, Acting Proctor Prof Md Azam Khan told The Daily Star that the university administration had verbally withdrawn the slaughter restriction and permitted the program on the condition that the slaughter be conducted hygienically under university supervision. Khan stated that university officials and police monitored the event and that no untoward incident occurred."
    }
  ]
}

