[
  {
    "text": "Masked Israeli settlers wielding clubs, slingshots, and other weapons attacked Palestinian olive farmers and a Washington Post photographer in the occupied West Bank on Friday, October 15, 2026. The coordinated movement, described as part of yearly attacks to disrupt the annual olive harvest, resulted in injuries to at least 20 people across eight villages, according to Palestinian authorities. Witnesses reported that settlers also burned cars and approximately a dozen olive trees during the violence. In the town of Abu Falah, near Ramallah, at least 80 settlers—some carrying firearms or clubs—gathered to stop harvesting. When some Palestinians used rocks to defend themselves, the Israeli military reportedly fired teargas in their direction. The Israeli Foreign Press Association stated that such violent attacks against journalists and civilians have been a recurring issue and that calls for action have been ignored by Israeli authorities. The United Nations noted that levels of settler violence have reached an all-time high this year, with the past two weeks seeing more attacks than the entire previous harvest season."
  },
  {
    "text": "Among those injured was Heidi Levine, a veteran photographer for The Washington Post, who was beaten during an attack that witnesses said lasted more than 10 minutes. Levine, who was wearing a protective vest clearly marked as “press,” reported being pushed to the ground and kicked. Settlers also reportedly stole her passport, phone, wallet, and camera equipment. Levine was treated at a hospital in Ramallah for injuries to her leg and hand. Washington Post Executive Editor Matt Murray denounced the attack as unwarranted and called for a thorough investigation. Reporters Without Borders characterized the incident as “brazen violence.” Local officials reported that among the Palestinian injuries were four dual Palestinian-American citizens, including Majdi Fheed, who sustained head and neck wounds. In response to the reports of violence, a spokesperson for the US State Department said they were aware of the situation."
  },
  {
    "text": "The violence occurred amid broader diplomatic tensions regarding West Bank settlements. On October 15, 2026, Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi met with Israeli President Isaac Herzog in Azerbaijan. During the meeting, Wang told Herzog that the “Palestinian question” is the “greatest historical injustice of this century” and urged Israel to stop the “forced establishment” of settlements in the West Bank. Herzog stated the meeting focused on Middle East relations and emphasized Israel's desire for peace alongside strategic security threats from Iran and its proxies."
  }
]}
